# taf.ul.alarms_watchdog

[![Documentation](https://docs.taf.ute.nsn-rdnet.net/projects/taf-ul-alarms_watchdog/badge/)][1]
[![Requirements](https://taf.wrgitlab-pages.int.net.nokia.com/miscellaneous/static-badges/requirements.svg)](requirements/base.txt)
[![build status](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.alarms-watchdog/badges/main/pipeline.svg)][2]
[![coverage report](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.alarms-watchdog/badges/main/coverage.svg)][2]
[![latest release](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.alarms-watchdog/-/badges/release.svg)][3]


Allows background watching of active alarms on BTS during test case execution



## Supported Python versions

Library should work properly with Python versions specified within
`python_requires` option in [setup.py](setup.py).

## Installation

Generic library installation process is described in [TAF user guide][4].

## Usage

First, import `alarms_watchdog` class and create it's instance in Robot file:

```robot
*** Settings ***
Library     taf.ul.alarms_watchdog
```

Now prepare list of allowed alarms which will be skipped during active alarms check. Keyword `create_alarm` can be used to populate list of allowed alarms. Single alarm should be defined by at least on of following attributes:
* ClassicalBts / RealEnb: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `hw_path`, `severity`, `reason`, `unit_path`, `impacted_cells_count`, `fault_signature`
* CloudBts: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `severity`, `unit_path`

In case of empty list all active alarms will be considered as disallowed.

```robot
*** Test Cases ***
Exemplary test case
    ${allowed_alarm_1}=    Create alarm
    ...                    alarm_id=7220
    ...                    fault_id=61524
    ...                    fault_name=SSH enabled

    ${allowed_alarm_2}=    Create alarm
    ...                    fault_name=Temp Fault1

    ${allowed_alarms}=     Create list
    ...                    ${allowed_alarm_1}
    ...                    ${allowed_alarm_2}
```

Prepare configuration of BTS to which requests for active alarms will be send (it should contain all data required to connect to given BTS). Currently following BTS configuration classes are supported:
* ClassicalBts
* CloudBts
* RealEnb

If no configuration will be provided then ClassicalBts with default connection details will be used.

To connect with gNB BTS:

```robot
    ${bts_config}=         ${tl.gnbs[0]}
```

To connect with eNB BTS:

```robot
    ${bts_config}=         ${tl.enbs[0]}
```

Then initiate background alarms watching with given time interval between consecutive requests to BTS for active alarms (default value is 2 seconds).

```robot
    Watch alarms           allowed_alarms=${allowed_alarms}
    ...                    interval=5 sec
    ...                    bts_config=${bts_config}
```

From now on a background thread will run, periodically collect and store active alarms from BTS which were not allowed on allowed alarms list. Test case execution will not be blocked by `Watch alarms` keyword - Robot Framework will proceed with remaining part of test case.
More details on how `Watch alarms` keyword is collecting disallowed alarms history can be found in Test Automation Framework library documentation: https://docs.taf.nsn-rdnet.net/taf.ul.alarms_watchdog/

At any point in time (even before calling `Watch alarms` keyword) `Allow alarm` and `Disallow alarm` keywords can be used to change content of allowed alarms list. This can be done to dynamically allow and disallow alarms during test case execution - different test steps will trigger different alarms on BTS which can be considered as allowed at given point in time. Single allowed or disallowed alarm should be defined by at least on of following attributes:
* ClassicalBts / RealEnb: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `hw_path`, `severity`, `reason`, `unit_path`, `impacted_cells_count`, `fault_signature`
* CloudBts: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `severity`, `unit_path`

```robot
    Allow alarm            fault_name=No X2 link available
    Recommission BTS
    Wait for on air
    Disallow alarm         fault_name=No X2 link available
```

To check whether any disallowed alarm was detected one of two keywords can be used: `Has detected alarms` (will return True if any disallowed alarm has been detected) or `Check detected alarms` (will raise `DisallowedAlarmsDetected` if any disallowed alarm has been detected).

```robot
    ${alarms_detected}=    Has detected alarms
    Should be false        ${alarms_detected}

    Check detected alarms
```

```
DisallowedAlarmsDetected:
2020-07-16 09:37:08.591738 DETECTED alarm_id: 7116, fault_id: 2002, hw_path: N/A, fault_name: Receiving failure in Optical Interface
2020-07-16 09:46:19.460507 LOST     alarm_id: 7116, fault_id: 2002, hw_path: N/A, fault_name: Receiving failure in Optical Interface
2020-07-16 09:59:52.153371 DETECTED alarm_id: 7107, fault_id: 7300, hw_path: N/A, fault_name: No X2 link available
```

To check whether given disallowed alarm was detected `Was alarm detected` keyword can be used. Disallowed alarm should be defined by at least on of following attributes:
* ClassicalBts / RealEnb: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `hw_path`, `severity`, `reason`, `unit_path`, `impacted_cells_count`, `fault_signature`
* CloudBts: `alarm_id`, `fault_id`, `fault_name`, `severity`, `unit_path`

```robot
    ${alarm_detected}=     Was alarm detected    fault_name=No X2 link available
    Should be false        ${alarm_detected}
```

Watching alarms should always be stopped, otherwise connection to BTS will not be closed, sub-thread will not be joined with main thread and remain active in operating system after test case execution is finished. This can be done by using `Stop watching alarms` keyword (preferably in `[Teardown]` section).

```robot
[Teardown]  Stop watching alarms
```

More information about other available keywords can be found in Test Automation Framework library documentation: https://docs.taf.nsn-rdnet.net/taf.ul.alarms_watchdog/

